## Configuration declaration: start here

`internal/resource/configuration.go` is the visible project configuration contract:
`Config` declares fields, `Defaults()` declares initial choices, and `Validate()`
checks pure rules. It composes the public `i18n.Settings` type without copying
its implementation. This project-owned package is unrelated to Fathomry's private
runtime resource machinery. It does not import loaders or adapters.

| Application field | Type | Initial default | Rule |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `i18n.default_locale` | string | `[[.DefaultLocale]]` | Nonempty language-script-region tag, <=64 bytes; unavailable translations fall back to English |

`configs/base.yaml` shows every currently supported application field. Selected
environment files override only changed fields; `{}` inherits base. Examples are
editable project values, not a second implementation of framework defaults.

To add a business setting, declare its field with an explicit `json` name in
`resource.Config`, set any default in `Defaults()` and add necessary pure validation.
Then supply its value in the desired environment document. No acquisition or
Provider code changes are needed. To additionally permit a process/dotenv override,
declare that binding in `internal/configuration/bindings.go`; this authorization
is intentional, not automatic environment discovery.

`internal/configuration` selects inputs and delegates loading into `resource.Config`.
Add settings only for implemented public capabilities; do not copy private SDK
options, connections or clients into the declaration.
`internal/localization` owns embedded project resources. `cmd/[[.Name]]` is
the thin process entry, not the owner of configuration acquisition.

## Values, overrides and private dotenv files

Precedence: typed defaults < base < selected environment < explicitly authorized
local layer < declared variables. Variables prefer the process environment over
the explicitly selected dotenv file, including an empty process value.

```sh
cp .env.development.example .env.development
chmod 600 .env.development
./bin/[[.Name]] --environment development --env-file .env.development
```

Edit copied values before using a remote environment. `.env.example` is the
development example under a conventional filename. **No dotenv file is loaded
automatically.** Production cannot silently inherit a developer's `.env`.
Relative paths resolve from `--root`; absolute secret-mount paths are accepted.
A selected missing, invalid or oversized file fails. Private dotenv files are
ignored by Git, while examples contain no real credentials.

Only environment-prefixed bindings in `bindings.go` and selected source bootstrap
are consumed, for example `[[.Prefix]]_DEVELOPMENT_I18N_DEFAULT_LOCALE`.
Other names are not implicitly applied. Every dotenv assignment must still be
syntactically valid. Dotenv is literal KEY=VALUE input, not a shell script:
no variable expansion, command substitution or process-environment mutation.
Duplicate names fail; multiline values use double-quoted escapes such as `\n`.
Process and dotenv origins appear in safe variable metadata without names/values.

`--local` opts into the selected environment's optional Local layer. It is a
precedence layer, not a request to switch a remote environment to local files.
Missing optional input may be absent; invalid/denied/unavailable input still fails.

[[if .Local]]### Local / Viper

Local environments read `configs/base.yaml` and
`configs/environments/<environment>.yaml`. The optional Local layer reads
`configs/local/<environment>.yaml`. Copy the example before opting in.
Files are explicit; no whole-directory scan or cross-environment merge occurs.
[[end]]
[[if .Remote]]### Remote / Nacos

Remote environments use the bootstrap names in their dotenv examples, or matching
process variables. HTTP and gRPC endpoints, credentials and TLS trust must be
supplied before fetching application settings; they cannot come from Nacos itself.
No file fallback, cached configuration, watch or implicit namespace inference exists.

Default group is DEFAULT_GROUP. The base data ID is `[[.Name]].base.yaml`;
environment ID is `[[.Name]].<environment>.yaml`; optional Local ID is
`[[.Name]].<environment>.local.yaml`. Override through the explicit Nacos bindings
in `sources_nacos.go`. Files in `configs/` are reviewed example content to publish
through your authorized deployment process. This project never publishes them.

The generated bootstrap supports one explicit server. Names below have prefix
`[[.Prefix]]_<ENVIRONMENT>_NACOS_`; their full forms are in each remote dotenv example.

| Suffix | Input and default |
| --- | --- |
| `HTTP_URL` | Required HTTP(S) authentication origin; no implicit endpoint discovery |
| `GRPC_ADDRESS` | Required explicit host:port; not derived from HTTP |
| `NAMESPACE` | Empty selects the native default namespace |
| `GROUP` | `DEFAULT_GROUP` |
| `BASE_ID` | `[[.Name]].base.yaml` |
| `ENVIRONMENT_ID` | `[[.Name]].<environment>.yaml` |
| `LOCAL_ID` | `[[.Name]].<environment>.local.yaml`, used only with `--local` |
| `USERNAME`, `PASSWORD` | Both present or both absent; no real credentials in examples |
| `ROOT_CA_PEM` | Explicit trust bundle, or empty for system roots |
| `ALLOW_INSECURE` | JSON boolean, default false; true explicitly permits insecure transport |

Other supported adapter options remain explicit Go declarations in `sources_nacos.go`,
not hidden environment bindings. Add bindings deliberately if the project needs them.

Setting HTTPS does not by itself secure the separate gRPC channel; provide trust
and leave ALLOW_INSECURE=false outside explicitly authorized isolated testing.
[[end]]
## Internationalization

Generated bootstrap language is [[.DefaultLocale]]. Effective
`i18n.default_locale` selects project output after loading. `--lang` overrides
one invocation without changing configuration; creation-tool `--lang` does not
choose the generated project's default. English source and Chinese translations
are embedded explicitly; valid unsupported languages use the existing English
fallback. Help/startup failures need no local or remote configuration.

## Configuration troubleshooting

- Unknown environment or invalid declarations: check `sources.go`, command arguments
  and the selected bootstrap example. A missing required variable is not a default.
- Unavailable input: check required local documents, the explicitly selected dotenv
  file, or the remote service, authentication and keys. There is no silent fallback.
- Invalid data: check field names in `resource.Config`, types, literal dotenv syntax
  and JSON encoding for non-text variable bindings. Overridden invalid input may
  still be rejected; do not use a higher layer to hide malformed lower input.
- Invalid locale: inspect `i18n.default_locale` and its selected variable binding;
  an empty process value intentionally overrides a nonempty file value and fails.

Failures preserve a stable code and provide localized guidance without printing
private paths, variable names, credentials or raw parser/service diagnostics.

## Patched dependencies and limits

If a selected SDK bundle was supplied, `third_party/fathomry/manifest.json`
records the version-bound sources and root `go.mod` has project-relative
replacements. Preserve notices and provenance. Content hashes are not producer
authentication, compatibility qualification or license clearance.
`go mod verify` does not verify local replacement contents.

This is editable source, not a managed project-regeneration engine. Do not rerun
creation over this directory. Configuration success is not service readiness or
a frozen Run fact; output is unversioned diagnostic metadata, never settings.
